{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":15,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.normalisations_distinguishable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.normalisations_distinguishable","kind":"theorem","line":231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L231-L239","doc":"The residual above is nonzero exactly when the nucleons are not degenerate —\nso on the physical `m_p ≠ m_n` the two conventions are genuinely distinguishable,\nand the `1/4` cannot be transplanted between them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.directInterp_like_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.directInterp_like_pair","kind":"theorem","line":223,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L223-L229","doc":"The same for `directInterp` — the two agree on like pairs and differ only at\nthe unlike one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossInterp_like_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossInterp_like_pair","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L211-L221","doc":"**Both interpolations fix a like pair**, for any masses and any endpoint\nvalue — because a like pair averages a letter with itself.  This is the theorem\nform of `pn_interface_bar_structure_probe`'s *\"a like-flavour bond has no mean to\ntilt\"*, and it is what retires the \"mass fraction fails at the like pairs\"\nobjection recorded in an earlier version of this file's docstring: the three\nletters are one rule, not two. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.direct_sub_cross_eq_deltaMean_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.direct_sub_cross_eq_deltaMean_half","kind":"theorem","line":204,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L204-L209","doc":"**The two differ by exactly `δ/2`.**  So the choice between them is the sign\nof the colour tilt and nothing else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.directInterp_eq_reflected","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.directInterp_eq_reflected","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L195-L202","doc":"**The direct interpolation is the `δ ↦ −δ` reflection** — it gives\n`1/2 + δ/4`, the neutron's fraction, and is the alternative production does not\ntake. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossInterp_eq_tPN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossInterp_eq_tPN","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L186-L193","doc":"**The production `pn` weight is the CROSS interpolation**, exactly: with\n`t_p = 0` (the `pp` letter is `σ`) and `t_n = 1` (the `nn` letter is `σ⁻¹`),\n`crossInterp m_p m_n 0 1 = m_p/(m_p+m_n) = 1/2 − δ/4`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossInterp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossInterp","kind":"def","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L183-L184","doc":"See `directInterp`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.directInterp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.directInterp","kind":"def","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L177-L181","doc":"The two interpolations of the like-pair endpoint values `t_p`, `t_n` between\na nucleon of mass `ma` carrying `ta` and one of mass `mb` carrying `tb`:\n`directInterp` weights each endpoint by **its own** mass, `crossInterp` by **the\nother's**.  Production's `pn` letter is `crossInterp`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossWeights_sum_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.crossWeights_sum_one","kind":"theorem","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L171-L175","doc":"The two weights are convex: they sum to `1` for any nonzero total mass. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.wPP_mean_normalised","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.wPP_mean_normalised","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L154-L168","doc":"**And the weight on the `pp` letter is the NEUTRON's mass fraction.**\n\n`pn_interface_bar_structure_probe` fact 3 writes the canonical interface as\n`f_pn = w_p·f_pp + w_n·f_nn` with `w_p = 1/2 + δ/4`.  Together with\n`tPN_mean_normalised` (`w_n = 1/2 − δ/4 = m_p/(m_p+m_n)`) this says each\nlike-pair letter is carried by the mass fraction of **the other** nucleon:\n\n    σ_pn = (m_n·σ + m_p·σ⁻¹)/(m_p + m_n) .\n\nAn identity, not a derivation — see the docstring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.quarter_is_wrong_under_sum_normalisation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.quarter_is_wrong_under_sum_normalisation","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L142-L152","doc":"**And `1/4` is positively WRONG under sum-normalisation**, by a residual that\nvanishes only when the two masses agree.\n\nThis is what makes the two theorems above a statement rather than a\nre-labelling: the coefficient is not free to be either, it is determined by which\nmass normalises `δ`, and picking the wrong pairing is detectable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.tPN_sum_normalised","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.tPN_sum_normalised","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L131-L140","doc":"**Under sum-normalisation the coefficient is `1/2`, not `1/4`.**\n\nThe *same* physical weight — the proton's mass fraction — reads `1/2 − δ/2` when\n`δ` is normalised by `m_p + m_n`. So the numeral is carried by the convention,\nnot by the physics. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.tPN_mean_normalised","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.tPN_mean_normalised","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L118-L129","doc":"**The production `pn` weight is the proton's mass fraction — and the `1/4`\nis what the mean-normalisation costs.**\n\n`t_pn = 1/2 − δ/4 = m_p/(m_p+m_n)` identically, for `δ` normalised by the mean.\nThis is `BondLetterIdentification.tPN_eq_proton_mass_fraction` restated with the\nnormalisation made explicit, which is the point: the `4` is `2 × 2`, one factor\nfrom `M_N` being a mean and one from `Δm` being a full difference. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.deltaSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.deltaSum","kind":"def","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L113-L116","doc":"The same split normalised by the **sum** `m_p + m_n` instead — the other\nnatural reading of \"the pair's mass\", used here only as the contrast that makes\nthe coefficient falsifiable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.deltaMean","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.FlavourSplitNormalisation.deltaMean","kind":"def","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/FlavourSplitNormalisation.lean#L108-L111","doc":"The substrate flavour split as the corpus normalises it: `Δm / M_N` with\n`M_N` the **mean** nucleon mass `(m_p + m_n)/2`\n(`_li_2_q_util.flavor_split_leading`, `prop:flavor-split-substrate`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}